Day 06 – Docker Troubleshooting

Objective

Practiced investigating and resolving common Docker issues through three simulated company-style troubleshooting scenarios.

Scenario 1: Incorrect Port Mapping

- Investigated an Nginx container using "docker ps -a".
- Found that the container had no host port mapping.
- Removed the incorrectly configured container.
- Recreated it using "-p 9095:80".
- Verified that the Nginx welcome page opened successfully at "http://localhost:9095".

Scenario 2: Container Immediately Exits

- Investigated the container status using "docker ps -a".
- Found the container in the "Exited (1)" state.
- Used "docker logs" to identify the simulated error message.
- Learned how a terminated main process causes a container to stop.
- Understood the difference between exit code "0" (success) and "1" (failure).

Scenario 3: Incorrect Image Tag

- Reproduced an image pull failure using an invalid tag:
  "nginx:wrong-tag-123"
- Identified the incorrect tag in the image reference.
- Successfully pulled the correct image using:
  "sudo docker pull nginx:latest"
- Verified the downloaded image using "docker images".

Key Learnings

- Investigating container status and port mappings.
- Reading container logs to identify failures.
- Understanding container lifecycle and exit codes.
- Identifying incorrect image names and tags.
- Applying a systematic troubleshooting workflow.

Troubleshooting Workflow

Investigate → Identify Root Cause → Fix → Verify

Completed three practical Docker troubleshooting scenarios.
